<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="cartesian-general-regions-complete">
    <title>Integration Over General Regions (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        We've developed multiple integrals over rectangular regions. We'll see how to establish bounds
                        that represent more general regions (non-rectangular).
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>General Approach</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                      Look at your region in the <m>x-y</m> plane and determine if you can view it as a section between 
                      two functions of <m>x</m> or two functions of <m>y</m>:
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Refer to class notes for a picture of the two situations. If you region is between two functions
                        of <m>x</m>, <m>f_1(x)</m> and <m>f_2(x)</m> for <m>x</m> in <m>(a,b)</m>, the general form of 
                        your integral will be
                        <md>
                            \int_D \quad dA = \int_a^b \int_{f_1(x)}^{f_2(x)} \quad dydx
                        </md>.
                        If you region is between two functions
                        of <m>y</m>, <m>g_1(y)</m> and <m>g_2(y)</m> for <m>y</m> in <m>(c,d)</m>, the general form of 
                        your integral will be
                        <md>
                            \int_D \quad dA = \int_c^d \int_{g_1(y)}^{g_2(y)} \quad dxdy
                        </md>.
                        
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
            <exercise workspace="2in">
                <title>Example: General Regions</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Let <m>D</m> be the region below <m>y=1</m> and above <m>y=x</m> for positive <m>x</m>. We could integrate 
                        over this region in either order:
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        <md>
                            <mrow>\iint_D f(x,y) \quad dA \amp= \int_0^1 \int_x^1 f(x,y) \quad dydx </mrow>
                            <mrow> \amp= \int_0^1 \int_0^y f(x,y) \quad dxdy </mrow>
                        </md>
                        
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        If <m>f(x,y) = e^{y^2}</m>, only one of these orders is possible: <m>dxdy</m>. Integrating in this 
                        order gives a result of <m>\frac{1}{2}(e-1)</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
    
    </page>
    <page>

            <exercise workspace="2in">
                <title>Practice</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Compute <m>\displaystyle \iint_D 1 + 2xy \quad dA</m> in both orders, where <m>D</m> is the region
                        below <m>y=x</m> and above <m>y=x^2</m>.
                        
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        We could compute this integral as 
                        <md>
                            \int_0^1 \int_{x^2}^x 1 + 2xy \quad dy dx
                        </md>
                        or
                        <md>
                            \int_0^1 \int_{y}^{\sqrt{y}} 1 + 2xy \quad dx dy
                        </md>.
                        Either order should give a result of <m>\frac{1}{4}</m>.
                        
                    </p>
                </solution>
                
            </exercise>

    </page>

   

       
    </worksheet>
